#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1987" :question/problem "A computer has $10$ files infected with a computer virus. If the number of infected files quadruples every hour, find a function that gives the number of infected files after $t$ hours. How many infected files will there be after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of infected files, $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we  substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" :question/math-academy-id "e-1988" :question/problem "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week. \n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." :question/worked-solution "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny   = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4,$ we evaluate the above for $t=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21183" :question/problem "A podcast was released with $80$ initial listeners. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after release, the number of listeners increased by $190\\%$ of the number of listeners at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many listeners are there estimated to be after $2$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb4232f-556f-57a2-888b-432f46d7fa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488599} {:answer/id #uuid "bc41e08e-82a6-5684-be54-6391754b72d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488848} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d139d1-e0ec-52c5-9377-3fdeed0d175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488860} {:answer/id #uuid "e43f28f8-6ab9-5593-9ae7-16d8b9e7316a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488947} {:answer/id #uuid "e86c0f47-a4fd-56e0-8db9-ac7aded15688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e86c0f47-a4fd-56e0-8db9-ac7aded15688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488958} :answer-field/id #uuid "044468b2-bf94-5551-bde4-fd78c33e877e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3269c43-47df-5acc-97bf-5a6fd7c5c99e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103103" :question/problem "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ in a culture after $t$ hours is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\ny=\\text{___}\\cdot {5}^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the culture started with $7$ bacteria and this number increases fivefold every hour, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $5.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of the growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=7$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=5$ is the growth factor (the number of bacteria increases fivefold every hour).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\cdot {5}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "360767c6-d66d-5903-9180-b0da4415a5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488582} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca45b50-aa83-548f-8093-7884f91372f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1758" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488595} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3bbde6-9914-5283-a9d6-322d06b9ef0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "410" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488683} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1a9c65-fa8c-5d77-8312-c62177840ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1962" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488814} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6abb1b-3487-54e7-bdd5-09377dd6493c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca45b50-aa83-548f-8093-7884f91372f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1758" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488595} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4c7c93-453d-5bac-b606-f82c34cb377d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f781a71-78ec-54be-875d-95c07fb380d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-318214" :question/problem "An online course launched with $100$ initial students. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after launch, the number of students increased by $160%$ of the number of students at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many students are there estimated to be after $3$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "The number of students $S(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $160%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=100$ is the total number of students initially\n\n- $r=1.6$ is the growth rate ($160%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after the launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nS=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $S(t)$ is\n\n$$\nS=100\\cdot (1+1.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of students after $3$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nS(3)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{3}≈1758\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of students after $3$ weeks is $1758.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "907d6a8b-220d-5ffa-a86f-09363e7e2f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3859" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144304} {:answer/id #uuid "94778e61-ef88-5a09-9df7-8ee5df28bf58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3685" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144305} {:answer/id #uuid "ea98df17-9ef1-5762-bfe2-bd4e457076aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144306} {:answer/id #uuid "430728e2-b29f-55c2-a057-2541cf308593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3754" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144307} {:answer/id #uuid "46941793-8685-5dbd-ab7e-f7fa10af6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907d6a8b-220d-5ffa-a86f-09363e7e2f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3859" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb9ea82-bb25-57e8-9225-b8bc7341a2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66005c3f-ae8d-568e-8ad1-166663174f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-318220" :question/problem "A concert video was uploaded with $90$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $250%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $250%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=90$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.5$ is the growth rate ($250%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=90\\cdot (1+2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{3}≈3859\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $3859.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247e479c-9bbc-5bbe-a0e3-0aae8ea1b13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488545} {:answer/id #uuid "4d146dc1-99e1-5705-9fb0-9267e886095e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488621} {:answer/id #uuid "56e051a7-8f0b-5466-b6d8-f334c4a74b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488636} {:answer/id #uuid "fb429f52-dc4a-587c-9a61-ace3e5f5c296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489004} {:answer/id #uuid "fd891c3d-5a7a-5876-86e9-682f7695882e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d146dc1-99e1-5705-9fb0-9267e886095e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488621} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d48576-5f2c-576e-8f97-f11d75b1ee78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ef74434-74b6-5bd1-8d2d-3a97591fb1bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-61657" :question/problem "A computer has $20$ files infected with a computer virus. The number of infected files $y(t)$ after $t$ days is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny(t)=20\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the number of infected files increases $5%$ each day, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of infected files $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $5%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=20$ is the initial number of infected files.\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the growth rate ($5%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\cdot (1+0.05)^{t} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (1.05)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.05.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58af08ce-57e1-55c2-8835-a1c47b3d45c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=14\\cdot {1.15}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488638} {:answer/id #uuid "5f44ed35-a73d-59f9-a388-f6e2b4eae254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=14\\cdot {0.15}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488654} {:answer/id #uuid "62742e47-d5f9-5a77-94a3-f21b87271f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=14\\cdot {15}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488657} {:answer/id #uuid "6a39807f-e0b0-55ac-8c7b-183b06419467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=(14\\cdot 0.15)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488680} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa68ffa-c31a-55a5-8217-a253d0cfd3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=(14\\cdot 1.15)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58af08ce-57e1-55c2-8835-a1c47b3d45c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=14\\cdot {1.15}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488638} :answer-field/id #uuid "09241892-4d92-5242-9af7-710ba5f70453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1c43e4b-f0c3-5827-a9fe-03d9f0ad0749" :question/math-academy-id "q-61866" :question/problem "There are $14$ bacteria in a culture. Every minute, the number of bacteria increases by $15%.$ What function represents the number of bacteria $y(t)$ after $t$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=14$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the growth rate ($15%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =14\\cdot (1+0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =14\\cdot {1.15}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c42295e-f712-5a56-90ec-b9b3ab0ee9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\,000+{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488496} {:answer/id #uuid "14269be5-6f1d-50ca-9b8b-1b0e3e6814fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\,{000}^{t}+{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488514} {:answer/id #uuid "8654440e-5fa6-5947-bc7d-7eab8ff3173b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\cdot (2\\,000)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488732} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e651cf-8673-5d70-a5e0-df15771d9aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=(2\\,000\\cdot 2)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488788} {:answer/id #uuid "dce4d0f7-0a32-5960-a13f-40ad332e5baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dce4d0f7-0a32-5960-a13f-40ad332e5baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488928} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed8a07f-b1e3-5291-93c3-eed235b080d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d03e717-deaf-523f-a995-dad5a2ef0024" :question/math-academy-id "q-63824" :question/problem "At the beginning of a study, there were $2\\,000$ ants in an anthill. The study shows that the number of ants doubles every month. What function represents the number of ants $y(t)$ after $t$ months?" :question/worked-solution "The number of ants $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of ants.\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor (the number of ants doubles every month).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$"}]}
